  "transcript": "# Children’s social care\n\n## The overall picture: failing too many children\n> Dame Rachel says children’s social care still fails many children—high-profile tragedies and quieter, widespread harms show the system is not delivering.\n\nChair: Dame Rachel, to start: is the children’s social care system meeting needs and delivering good outcomes, especially for children with complex needs or deprived of their liberty?\nDame Rachel de Souza: No. There are high-profile avoidable deaths and many quieter failures. Children are in illegal homes, education outcomes are poor, care leavers face homelessness, and we have children stuck in hospital or prison because services do not join up.\nDame Rachel de Souza: I published a large set of child metrics called 'Childhood Lottery' showing huge variation by area: some places do much worse on placements, social worker turnover and support for care leavers.\nDame Rachel de Souza: I’m frustrated that many so-called reforms are ideas on paper without delivery. The system needs stronger accountability, data and focused action.\nChair: You mentioned prisons and mental health provision—do you work closely with the CQC on specialist mental health services for children?\nDame Rachel de Souza: There are good people in both inspectorates, but we need Ofsted and CQC to work much more closely with social care so specialist provision is planned before children are placed there.\n\n\n## Illegal, unregistered homes: scale, cost and solutions\n> About 669 children are in unregistered homes; Dame Rachel calls the practice illegal, expensive and avoidable, and demands faster registration and local planning.\n\nLord Barber of Chittlehampton: You said 669 children were in illegal homes and it cost £350 million last year. Why is this happening and what should the DfE do?\nDame Rachel de Souza: It’s partly lack of capacity and poor planning. But illegal is illegal: councils and Ofsted should not pass inspections if children are placed in illegal homes. Registration delays must be fixed and areas must plan five-year strategies for beds and specialist foster care.\nDame Rachel de Souza: Some placements cost £10,000 a week or even £1 million a year for individual children, often in caravans or B&Bs. That money could be used to build proper local provision or support families.\nLord Barber of Chittlehampton: Directors say they have to use illegal placements at short notice. You’re saying they should have planned for that before the crisis?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Yes. Strategic planning and sufficiency of local placements are practical and doable. If each MP took one child from the backlog, it would show how small numbers actually are—this is solvable.\nBaroness Watkins of Tavistock: Some local authorities have ready homes but can’t get Ofsted to inspect them quickly. Could councils inspect each other or use a neutral body?\nDame Rachel de Souza: I’m open to pragmatic solutions, but beware unintended consequences. The DfE used to register free schools and had capacity; a neutral, trusted route for quicker sign-off could help if it’s done carefully.\n\n\n## Inspection, profit and the role of regulators\n> Dame Rachel welcomes Ofsted action on illegal homes but criticises slow registration, widespread private-profit provision and asks for clearer inspection standards.\n\nLord Mohammed of Tinsley: Ofsted may grade a home as 'good' yet outcomes for looked-after children remain poor. How do we fix that?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Ofsted’s new framework could help if it focuses on outcomes, inclusion and stops accepting minimum standards. Inspectors need the courage to draw lines—particularly over illegal placements.\nLord Mohammed of Tinsley: Should registration and inspection be separated? Do current Ofsted responsibilities cause a problem?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Separation can work: schools were registered by DfE and inspected by Ofsted. The immediate problem is delays in registering homes—hurry up and register them and don’t accept illegal placements.\nDame Rachel de Souza: Another issue is private profit. A small number of companies run most children’s homes and have made large profits. I don’t think profit should be driving placements where children are vulnerable.\nLord Barber of Chittlehampton: You used to run schools—how do you see regulation here compared with education reforms?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Education has shown reform can work with clear accountability and data. Children’s social care needs the same focus, and regulators must use data and standards to drive change.\n\n\n## Data, unique ID and joined-up services\n> Dame Rachel argues a national unique identifier and better data sharing between health, education, social care and justice would prevent children from slipping through the gaps.\n\nLord Barber of Chittlehampton: You keep returning to unique IDs and data systems. Should creating that be a top recommendation?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Yes. A single identifier would let professionals see a child’s whole history and stop repeated missed opportunities—Prevent referrals, hospital stays, or children off school who aren’t known to services.\nBaroness Nichols of Selby: People hide behind data protection. Why can’t agencies share data that would protect children?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Many are allowed to share but lack the systems and courage. Local examples show police or health teams find children not on any roll. A digitised 'red book' for each child, with safeguards, would transform care.\nChair: Could the single patient record in the Health Bill be the vehicle for progress on a child unique ID?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Possibly. If social care can’t lead, health could, but it must be multi-agency and include justice. The goal is joined-up records for children across services.\n\n\n## Prevention, fostering, regional planning and next steps\n> She urges more family support, clearer foster recruitment messaging, regional planning for specialist placements, five-year strategies and true corporate parenting.\n\nBaroness Hollins: Could regional care co-operatives help supply placements and move children out of secure or long-stay psychiatric care?\nDame Rachel de Souza: They have big potential to plan and pool specialist beds and support families, but so far they’re mostly paper ideas. We must align footprints—health, education and local government—and deliver, not just pilot.\nBaroness Redfern: You welcomed powers to cap profit. How would that work?\nDame Rachel de Souza: In principle I’d avoid profit-making in placements and instead grow social enterprises and proper local provision. The law must be clear about what profits, if any, are acceptable and how to enforce caps.\nBaroness Nichols of Selby: Fostering is short of carers. Why don’t people foster now?\nDame Rachel de Souza: Many prospective carers are put off early by clumsy processes or being asked about finances first. Most children needing foster care today have complex needs. Recruitment needs a better public campaign and clearer step-in expectations and support.\nDame Rachel de Souza: Prevention matters: family hubs, early years and school-centred support work where they’re properly funded. We can avoid many care entries with sustained local family help.\nChair: Final question: what are the top practical steps you want to see now?\nDame Rachel de Souza: First, stop using illegal homes and speed up registration; second, plan capacity with five-year strategies and regional co-ops; third, create a functioning unique ID and shared records; fourth, back family support and foster recruitment; and fifth, insist on true corporate parenting across services.\nChair: Thank you. Your evidence has been clear and pressing. We appreciate your work and time today.\nDame Rachel de Souza: Thank you. I will share more specific numbers on things like long-term isolation on wards, and I’m happy to keep working with the committee.\n",
